          "import { useCallback, useEffect, useEffectEvent, useRef } from \"react\";",
          "  // INITIAL LOAD (loadChats is a no-op once chats are loaded or loading)\n  useEffect(() => {\n    loadChats();\n  }, [loadChats]);",
          "  const saveSettledChat = useEffectEvent(() => {\n    saveCurrentChat();\n  });\n\n  // Auto-save when generation completes: null while the last message is\n  // generating, otherwise changes when a message is added, removed or replaced\n  const settledMessagesKey = lastMessageInHistory?.isGenerating\n    ? null\n    : `${chatHistory.messages?.length}:${lastMessageInHistory?.id}`;\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (settledMessagesKey !== null) {\n      saveSettledChat();\n    }\n  }, [settledMessagesKey]);"
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I edited `useTTDChatStorage.ts` to fix all four issues. I couldn't re-lint it, because neither `oxlint` nor `node_modules` is installed in this directory, so the fixes are reasoned from the rule output rather than tested.\n\n**Issues 1 and 3 (the initial-load effect):** I added `loadChats` to the dependency list, as the rule's help text suggests. `loadChats` already returns early once chats are loaded or loading, so extra runs do nothing. The one difference: if some other code sets the exported `chatsLoadedAtom` back to `false`, chats now reload, where before they were loaded only once, on mount.\n\n**Issues 2 and 4 (the auto-save effect):** Adding `saveCurrentChat` to the dependencies would have changed behavior. It changes whenever anything in `chatHistory` changes, so the chat would be saved on every keystroke in the prompt. If the parent passes in a new adapter object on every render, it could also loop. Instead:\n- `saveCurrentChat` is now called through `useEffectEvent`, which is available in React 19.3. It always uses the latest chat but doesn't trigger the effect itself.\n- The old dependencies (message count, last message id) were flagged as unnecessary because the effect body never used them. I replaced them with one value the body does use: `settledMessagesKey`. It is `null` while the last message is generating, and otherwise it combines the message count and the last message's id.\n\nThe auto-save still fires in the same cases as before: when a message is added, removed or replaced, and when generation finishes. One gap remains: before, a save also fired if the last message's `isGenerating` changed from unset to `false` with nothing else changing. That case shouldn't come up, since assistant messages start out generating and user messages never change it.",
        "understood": {
